 missing value, a `move` of a value into one of its own children, and so on), `applyPatch` throws a
 `JsonPatchError` and returns nothing, so a failed patch never leaves a partial result behind.
 
+`createPatch(from, to)` is the reverse: it returns the JSON Patch that turns `from` into `to`, so that a client
+can send only what changed instead of the whole new version:
+
+```ts
+import { createPatch } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+createPatch({ users: [{ name: 'Ada' }], version: 1 }, { users: [{ name: 'Ada' }, { name: 'Grace' }], version: 2 });
+// [{ op: 'add', path: '/users/1', value: { name: 'Grace' } }, { op: 'replace', path: '/version', value: 2 }]
+```
+
+The patch describes the differences and nothing more: members and elements that are equal are not repeated, and
+a change deep in a document is a single operation on its path. Objects are compared member by member, whatever
+the order of their keys. Arrays are aligned on their longest common subsequence of equal elements, so an element
+that is inserted, removed or shifted costs one operation rather than one per following element. Two elements
+that are not equal and sit in the same place are compared as a pair, so a changed element inside an object is
+reported as a change inside it. A value that changes type (an object that becomes an array, say) is replaced as a
+whole. Alignment is skipped for arrays too large for it (more than a million pairs of elements); the elements that
+differ then are paired by position, which is still correct but may describe more operations than necessary. Equal
+documents give an empty patch. `from` and `to` are never modified, and the values of the patch are copies that
+share nothing with them. Both must be JSON values, otherwise `createPatch` throws a `JsonPatchError`.
+
